# C# Service Layer Dependencies

## CRITICAL: Avoid Service-to-Service Dependencies

Services should NOT depend on other services to prevent circular dependency issues and maintain clean architecture.

## ❌ WRONG - Service depending on other services

```csharp
public class InvitationService : IInvitationService {
    private readonly ISessionService _sessionService;      // BAD!
    private readonly IPasswordService _passwordService;    // BAD!

    public InvitationService(
        MainApiDbContext dbContext,
        ISessionService sessionService,
        IPasswordService passwordService
    ) {
        _dbContext = dbContext;
        _sessionService = sessionService;
        _passwordService = passwordService;
    }
}
```

## ✅ CORRECT - Services are injected into handlers

```csharp
// Service layer - only depends on DbContext and infrastructure
public class InvitationService : IInvitationService {
    private readonly MainApiDbContext _dbContext;
    private readonly ILogger<InvitationService> _logger;

    public InvitationService(
        MainApiDbContext dbContext,
        ILogger<InvitationService> logger
    ) {
        _dbContext = dbContext;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    // Service methods return data, don't orchestrate other services
    public async Task<User> CreateUserFromInvitationAsync(
        Invitation invitation,
        string firstName,
        string lastName,
        string passwordHash  // Handler passes hashed password
    ) {
        var user = new User {
            Email = invitation.Email,
            Password = passwordHash,  // Already hashed by handler
            FirstName = firstName,
            LastName = lastName,
            Status = UserStatus.Active,
            IsVerified = true
        };

        await _dbContext.User.AddAsync(user);
        await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();

        return user;
    }
}

// Handler orchestrates multiple services
public static class AcceptInvitation {
    public static async Task<Results<...>> HandleAcceptInvitation(
        [FromServices] IInvitationService invitationService,
        [FromServices] ISessionService sessionService,
        [FromServices] IPasswordService passwordService,
        [FromServices] IAuditLogService auditLogService,
        // ... other params
    ) {
        // Handler orchestrates - calls services in sequence
        var passwordHash = passwordService.HashPassword(password);
        var user = await invitationService.CreateUserFromInvitationAsync(
            invitation,
            firstName,
            lastName,
            passwordHash
        );
        var session = await sessionService.CreateSessionForUser(user);
        await auditLogService.LogAsync(...);

        return TypedResults.Ok(...);
    }
}
```

## Architecture Principle

**Handlers orchestrate, Services implement.**

- **Handlers**: Inject multiple services, orchestrate business workflows, handle cross-service transactions
- **Services**: Single responsibility, depend only on DbContext and infrastructure (Logger, Config, etc.)
- **Transactions**: Can be handled in handlers OR in services, but NOT across service boundaries

## When Services Need Shared Logic

If multiple services need the same functionality (like password hashing), extract it to a **utility class** or **static helper**, not a service:

```csharp
// ✅ CORRECT - Static utility for shared logic
public static class PasswordHasher {
    public static string HashPassword(string password) {
        // Hashing logic
    }
}

// Services use the utility directly
public class UserService {
    public async Task<User> CreateUserAsync(string email, string password) {
        var user = new User {
            Email = email,
            Password = PasswordHasher.HashPassword(password)  // Static utility
        };
        await _dbContext.User.AddAsync(user);
        return user;
    }
}
```

## Exception: Infrastructure Services

Infrastructure services (ILogger, IConfiguration, IOptions) are OK to inject into any service since they don't contain business logic and won't create circular dependencies.
